= Introduction 
\
Artificial Intelligence has been shaped by two  competing ideas, regarding where intelligence come from. The first, now being referred to as _Good Old fashioned AI_, held that intelligence is the manipulation of symbols according to explicit rules @emma2025 . Symbolic systems represent knowledge as facts and logical rules written by people, and reaches conclusions by inference. Expert systems such as MYCIN, which diagnosed bacterial infections from hand-coded medical rules , showed that this approach could match the specialists in some narrow domains @emma2025. Its strength was transparency : each conclusion can be traced back to a set of rules that produced it, during inference. Its weakness was brittleness: as mentioned above, rules had to be written and maintained by hand, so they failed on input that their authors had not anticipated. They also did not work directly with noisy perceptual data such as images or speech. 
\
\
The second idea held that intelligence could be learned from data as opposed to being programmed. Neural networks, which are loosely modelled on the brain, adjust the strength of connections between simple units to fit examples. The backpropagation algorithm made it practical to train networks with several layers @rumelhart1986. From the  early 2010s the combination of large datasets, fast hardware and deeper architectures produced the deep learning revolution @lecun2015. Deep networks can now recognise images, transcribe speech and generate text at a level symbolic systems never reached , and they do so without anyone ever writing down the rules for each specific case in the domain. 
\
\
Reinforcement learning(RL)  followed the same path. In RL , an agent learns by trial and error , taking actions in an environment and adjusting its behaviour to maximise a reward signal.  Combined with deep networks , RL produced agents that learn to play Atari games directly from pixels alone @mnih2015 and a system that defeated the a world champion at Go. @silva2021. These results provided sufficient evidence that a single learning algorithm , given enough experience , could mastyer complex sequential decision problems from raw input data. 
\
The properties that make deep RL so powerful also make it difficult to rely on outside the benchmarks where it succeeds. Four  major limitations have been identified throughout the literature. 
\
The first is *sample inefficiency*.Deep RL agents need enormous amounts of experience. Acharya et al. note that Rainbow DQN required roughly 83 hours of play to reach its reported Atari performance, a level many people reach within minutes @acharya2023. In simulation this is a cost in compute; in robotics, healthcare or any setting where experience is slow, expensive or dangerous to collect, it can rule the approach out entirely.

The second is *opacity*: Deep RL policy is distributed across millions of weights , and nothing in it corrosponds to a reason a person could inspect. Post-hoc explanations methods  try to recover such reasons after training, but Rudin argues that these explanations are only approximations of the models rather than complete faithful accounts of what it computes, and that in high-stakes domains models should be interpretable by design instead @rudin2019. 

The third is the *absence pf guarantees*. An RL agents cannot be simply told a rule such as "never enter this state". Such constrains are enconded indirectly through the reward, and nothing in the standard training preventrs the agent from violating the them while it explores @yang2023. Khandelwal et al. point to a relation problem in deployment, when a safety rule changes or a  new operating constraint is introduced , the usual response is to retrain o fine-tune the policy, which is costly, hard to audit and gives no assurance that the resulting change in the behaviour reflect the intended new rule requirements rather than  incidental patterns in the new data @khandelwal2026.

The fourth is *poor generalisation*. Agents trained in one setting often fail when the environment changes in ways a person would consider minor. On modified versions of three Atari games, for example, a standard neural PPO agent scored zero in every game, while an agent that combined neural and logic policies continued to perform @shindo2025.
\ \
_We acknowledge that these limitations are not unique to the absence of symbolic knowledge, and they are not permanent. Reinforcement learning is an active and fast-moving field, and each of the four problems is also being addressed by purely neural means, including model-based and meta-learning methods for sample efficiency and constrained optimisation for safety @altman1999 @khandelwal2026. Some of the limitations described here may therefore be reduced, or solved outright, without any symbolic component. The same caution applies to the neurosymbolic systems reviewed in later chapters: an improvement reported for a neurosymbolic agent shows that the combination works, not that the symbolic part was necessary. Whether symbolic knowledge offers advantages that neural methods cannot match is one of the questions this review sets out to examine (RQ3)_.
\
\
These limitations share a common cause. A purely neural agent learns *what* to do in the situations it has seen, but it has no explicit representation of *why*: no rules it can reuse, check, explain or have corrected. That is exactly what symbolic AI provided.
\
Symbolic and nueral approaches have almost opposite profiles. Symbolic systems are interpretable by construction, explainable, encode expert knowledge directly, reason about symbolic objects and relations, support long-horizon planning and littl or no training data, but they cannpt ground their symbols in raw perception. THis last gap, about how a symbol "door" to come to refer to anything in a camera image is the the long-standing  symbol grounding problem @harnad1990. Nueral systems handle perception so well but offer non of the other properties, which some domains , as we will analyse later, need them. 
\

Neurosymbolic AI starts from the observation that these profiles are complement each other, and that the most capable systems will combine both @garcez2023 @kautz2022. One way of viewing this combination, is by Sheth el al. whom frames it using the Kahnenman's two systems of thought: nueral networks provide fast,intuitive pattern recognition (system 1), while the symbolic knowledge supports the slow, deliberate reasoning (system 2) @sheth2023. They argue that integrating the two helps at two levels. At the algorithm level , symbolic structure supports abstraction, analogy,and long-term planning.In this context, absraction refers to treating one specific situation as a general concept, when needed. Analogy refers to the concept of recognising that a new situation has  the same relational structure as a familiar one, and then reusing what was learnt. Long-term planning involve reasoning about a sequence of steps towards a distant goal, such as getting the key, then opening the door, then reaching the exit, instead of reacting one step at a time. These capabilities are difficult to obtain from data alone. At application level, Symbolic knowledge supports explainability, interpretability and safety,because knowledge can constrain a model duirng training rather than only being used to explain it afterwards. 

The field has grown quickly. In a systematic review of neurosymbolic AI research from 2020 to 2024, Colelough and Regli screened 1,428 papers and analysed 167 with available code @colelough2024. Most of this work concentrates on learning and inference (63%), knowledge representation (44%) and logic and reasoning (35%). Explainability and trustworthiness are less represented (28%), and meta-cognition, the ability of a system to monitor and adjust its own reasoning, is barely studied (5%) @colelough2024.

Recent works show how varied the integration of Neural and Symbolic parts are. In knowledge discovery, Aerial+ trains an autoencoder on tabular data, and then extracts logical association rules from the trained network, producing smaller, higher quality rule sets than conventional rule-mining.Here the neural model serves as a means of finding symbolic knowledge.  In SymbolicAI, the direction is reversed: large language models act as semantic parsers that translate natural-language instructions into operations, which are then passed to symbolic solvers and composed into larger programs @dinu2024. ASPBench @szeider2026aspbench, uses a feedback-loop to LLMs to generate Answer Set Programming (ASP) rules.ASP is used complex, NP-hard combinatorial search and knowledge representation problems. RLSF also shows that reinforcement learning itself can serve as the bridge, ny fine-tuning language models using feedback from symbolic tools such as compilers and solvers, which return certificates pinpointing errors in the model's output. This gives the model fine-grained, sound reward signals instead of a single score from a learned reward model, and the authors report large gains over supervised fine-tuning, including a 52.64% improvement in compilation accuracy for program synthesis @jha2024.
\
\
Reinforcement Learning (RL) is where the case for combining neural and symbolic methods is strongest. An RL agent does not classify an input; it acts, and it mistakes accumulate over  a sequence of decisions. Every sample costs an interaction with the environmentm and the unsafe actions can cause real damage. AT the same time, RL tasks often contain structure that symbols capture naturally,like objects and the relations between them, subgoals that must be completed in order, the rules of a game, and the constraints of a safety specification. The four limitations above map directly onto the strengths of symbolic methods: structured knowledge can reduce the data an agent needs, logical policies can be read, constraints can be enforced, and abstract rules can transfer between environments. 
This idea originates from Garnelo et al. who proposed deep symbolic RL, in which a neural network maps raw input to a symbolic representation over which a symbolic learner operates @garnelo2016. Since then, symbolic components have been introduced into almost every part of the RL loop. Reward machines that express the reward function as a finite-state machine over high-level events, exposing the structure of a task to the agent @toroicarte2022. NUDGE learns policies as weighted logic rules, so that an agent's behaviour can be read as statements such as "jump when an enemy is close" @delfosse2023. BlendRL runs a neural and a logic policy side by side and learns when to rely on each @shindo2025. The Detect, Understand, Act framework selects its high-level actions with an Answer Set Program learned from only seven training arenas @mitchener2022. Khandelwal et al. put forth that an RL agent should consult an external, editable knowledge graph of rules, hard constraints and soft preferences at execution time, so that a change in requirements becomes an edit to the graph, enforced immediately by action shielding, rather than a retraining run @khandelwal2026.
\
Surveys have begun to organise this work. Yu et al. classify neural-symbolic systems by the direction in which the two components help each other: learning that serves reasoning, reasoning that serves learning, and approaches in which the two cooperate @yu2023. Acharya et al. apply this taxonomy to RL and catalogue existing systems by architecture @acharya2023.

Existing surveys either cover neurosymbolic AI in general @yu2023 @colelough2024 or catalogue neurosymbolic RL systems by architecture @acharya2023. Less attention has been paid to how specific RL methods change when symbolic components are added, and to whether the evidence supports the advantages that are claimed. This review addresses three research questions:

/ RQ1: How are established RL methods and components, such as the state representation, the policy, the reward function and exploration, transformed into neurosymbolic counterparts?

/ RQ2: In which application domains are these methods used, and what properties of those domains favour them?

/ RQ3: Does the reported evidence show that neurosymbolic RL is preferable to conventional deep RL, and if so, under what conditions?

The first is search-based agents in the style of AlphaGo @silver2016, where tree search operates over raw game states without explicit domain knowledge. Acharya et al. include such systems @acharya2023; here they are treated as deep RL combined with search. The second is post-hoc explanation methods that never feed their symbols back into the agent, since the symbolic output plays no role in how the agent learns or acts. The third is agents built on large language models, unless the language model produces or consumes symbolic artefacts that the RL loop then uses, as in BlendRL's rule generation @shindo2025 or RLSF's symbolic feedback @jha2024.

... introduces the necessary background on RL and symbolic representations. ... compares existing taxonomies of neurosymbolic integration. ... reviews the main method families, organised by the RL component that the symbolic part changes (RQ1). ... surveys application domains (RQ2), and ... critically assesses the evidence for and against neurosymbolic RL (RQ3). ... and ... cover recent trends and open problems.